 31| /**
 32|  * Representation of a Complex number, i.e. a number which has both a
 33|  * real and imaginary part.
 34|  * <br/>
 35|  * Implementations of arithmetic operations handle {@code NaN} and
 36|  * infinite values according to the rules for {@link java.lang.Double}, i.e.
 37|  * {@link #equals} is an equivalence relation for all instances that have
 38|  * a {@code NaN} in either real or imaginary part, e.g. the following are
 39|  * considered equal:
 40|  * <ul>
 41|  *  <li>{@code 1 + NaNi}</li>
 42|  *  <li>{@code NaN + i}</li>
 43|  *  <li>{@code NaN + NaNi}</li>
 44|  * </ul>
 45|  * Note that this is in contradiction with the IEEE-754 standard for floating
 46|  * point numbers (according to which the test {@code x == x} must fail if
 47|  * {@code x} is {@code NaN}). The method
 48|  * {@link org.apache.commons.math.util.Precision#equals(double,double,int)
 49|  * equals for primitive double} in {@link org.apache.commons.math.util.Precision}
 50|  * conforms with IEEE-754 while this class conforms with the standard behavior
 51|  * for Java object types.
 52|  * <br/>
 53|  * Implements Serializable since 2.0
 54|  *
 55|  * @version $Id$
 56|  */
